   the literal `go test -count=1 ./internal/handlers/accounts` line.
   Reproduced the exact PATH-frozen node-deletion scenario end to end in the
   scratch copy, in a single persistent shell (env resets between separate
   Bash tool calls, which matters for a PATH-keyed cache test): built a
   fixed PATH dir standing in for /usr/bin, ran `go test` with node present
   to warm the cache (real PASS, not -count=1), deleted the node symlink
   from that dir without touching PATH, then:
     - plain `go test ./internal/handlers/accounts/...` (no -count=1)
       replayed `ok ... (cached)` — the hole is real and reproduced.
     - `make test` (real invocation, not -n): exit 2, second recipe line
       fails with "node not found on PATH", `make: *** [Makefile:187: test]
       Error 1`.
     - `make test-unit`: exit 2, same failure.
     - `make race`: exit 2, same failure (~3 min build, ran to completion).
     - `make test-coverage`: exit 2, same failure, after all other packages
       report real `ok` results (cache for the rest of the suite intact).
   Converse checked: in `make test`'s recipe the FIRST `go test ./...`
   passed (cached) and did NOT mask the second line's genuine failure; make
   aborted the recipe on the second line's non-zero exit as expected. No
   masking either direction.
   `make -n check` shows `check`'s `test` prerequisite pulls in the same
   rerun line, so README's claim that `make check` also closes the hole
   holds.

   Oracle-design note (asked for explicitly): `make -n`, by itself, is not
   proof of enforcement in general — a `-`-prefixed recipe line, a trailing
   `|| true`, or a false `ifeq` branch would all still satisfy a
   text-pattern check against `-n` output while not enforcing anything. The
   accept.sh 3b check is therefore a weak proxy in isolation. It happens to
   be correct here only because I independently confirmed, by actually
   executing (not dry-running) all four targets under the adversarial
   scenario, that the recipe lines are real, unguarded, unswallowed
   commands. This is a property of the current Makefile text, not something
   the oracle itself proves — worth the record's note that a future edit
   adding `-` or `|| true` to that line would still pass 3b's `make -n`
   check.

